00:02:24.979 --> 00:02:29.159
The Owen Sound Current says the county describes him as this year's only recipient.

00:02:29.639 --> 00:02:34.179
The award is for leadership, innovation, and contributions to parametizen in Ontario.

00:02:34.679 --> 00:02:38.159
He has been with Gray County more than 30 years, nine of them is chief.

00:02:38.759 --> 00:02:42.899
A county release says he steered paramedic services toward a standard for community-based

00:02:42.899 --> 00:02:46.659
parametizen, medical innovation, and care that connects cleanly.

00:02:46.659 --> 00:02:51.619
The Community Paramedicine program started as a trial and is now a service the county calls

00:02:51.619 --> 00:02:54.999
essential, helping hundreds of people who live with chronic conditions.

00:02:55.479 --> 00:02:59.099
Those paramedics treat people at home, not only on emergency calls.

00:02:59.639 --> 00:03:04.039
Provincial grants he obtained keep the program going without a new bill for municipal taxpayers.

00:03:04.519 --> 00:03:09.579
His other work covers paramedic training, clinical guidelines used across Ontario, and health

00:03:09.579 --> 00:03:12.699
strategies the county credits with better care and gray and beyond.

00:03:12.699 --> 00:03:20.879
In the COVID-19 pandemic, that same direction meant local testing and vaccination outreach for people at high risk, including visits at home.

00:03:21.399 --> 00:03:27.079
Warden Andrea Matrosov said his leadership has deeply changed health care in Gray County and past its borders.

00:03:27.559 --> 00:03:35.019
She said he will not settle for how things already work, and that he keeps hunting for better care, better support for paramedics, and a stronger system.

00:03:35.019 --> 00:03:40.059
She said innovation, collaboration, and advocacy made the county a leader in community

00:03:40.059 --> 00:03:43.639
